-- Writing back into a `&` parameter is not letting it escape.
--
-- Compiled with specialization declined (see the `.generic` marker), because the false positive this
-- pins only exists in the erased form: a call whose callee the body cannot see conservatively
-- carries every argument's provenance into its result, so the value written back below is rooted in
-- `self`. Specialize the same source and the call becomes a known one whose result carries only what
-- it really came from, and nothing here reproduces.
--
-- The shape is `growJsArray` in `lib/Core/Array.js.yana`, which does `self.items = hostGrow(
-- self.items, wide)`, and it is mirrored rather than approximated. Three correct answers used to
-- compose into one wrong one: the destination is rooted in `self`, the value is rooted in `self`,
-- and `self` is a parameter and so outlives this frame - therefore `self` escaped. Nothing new
-- becomes reachable in that write; a root reached through *itself* was as reachable before it as
-- after.
--
-- Two details of the mirror are load-bearing, and both were found by writing the fixture wrong
-- first:
--
--  * **`advance` takes its argument by borrow, not `->`.** A handover of a part of `self` marks
--    `self` escaped through the *call*, which is a separate false positive with a separate cause -
--    so a `->` here would keep reporting after the fix and pin nothing.
--  * **`advance` is a class function.** What produces the conservative root is having no summary to
--    read: an ordinary generic function has a body that was resolved before the call was deferred,
--    so its result carries only what it came from. A deferred class dispatch names the class
--    signature, which has no body - the same case `hostGrow` is in by having no body at all.
--
-- Asserted through `.explain.expect`, which is the only dump that reads a *generic* body's summary:
-- the ownership dump prints specializations, and the specialization never had the bug. The row to
-- watch is `retains` on `step`, which must be `none`.
--
-- What this cost was not the one function. `growJsArray` reported that it kept `self`, `reserve`
-- inherited that from calling it and `push` inherited it from `reserve`, so one write took the whole
-- container API with it.

-- Native only, and `import Native` for the same reason LazyPointer.yana needs both: `%a` has no host
-- representation, so there would be nothing to emit on the managed target.
import Native

data Cursor(a) {at: %a}

class Advance(a):
  fn advance(return p: %a) -> %a

instance Advance(Int):
  fn advance(return p: %Int) = p

-- The write. `self.at` is replaced by something derived from `self.at`, which is one value's
-- lifetime through a borrow rather than two overlapping ones.
fn (Advance(a)) step(&self: Cursor(a)) -> {}:
    self.at = advance(self.at)

fn main() -> Int:
    let &cursor = Cursor {at: null()} :: Cursor(Int)
    step(cursor)
    return 0
